<h1>Can Email Reveal Who Outranks Whom?<br>Re-examining the Enron Hierarchy Benchmark</h1>
<div class="author">Jeb Farneth</div>
<div class="affil">Gabelli School of Business, Fordham University, New York, NY<br>
<a href="mailto:jf167@fordham.edu">jf167@fordham.edu</a> &middot; Code: <a href="https://github.com/jebfarneth/enron-knowledge-decay">github.com/jebfarneth/enron-knowledge-decay</a></div>
<div class="draft">WORKING DRAFT &middot; OCTOBER 2026 &middot; NOT FOR CIRCULATION</div>

<div class="abstract">
<h2>Abstract</h2>
<p>The Enron email corpus, with the organizational hierarchy that Agarwal et al. (2012) transcribed from Enron's
org charts, is the standard public benchmark for inferring who outranks whom from workplace email. We rebuild the
benchmark from the raw corpus in a tested, reproducible pipeline and ask what accuracy on it measures. Degree over
raw addresses reproduces the published network baseline (${raw_degree} of ${gold_pairs} dominance pairs, against
${published_degree} reported). On a main population of ${main_pairs} pairs, email-network measures order
${g_out_strength_all} to ${g_pagerank_all} of pairs correctly, and a mention network built with spaCy named-entity
recognition, after Agarwal et al. (2014), reaches ${g_mentioned_to_all} (paired difference with degree
${d_mentioned_to_all} points, 95% interval ${dci_mentioned_to_all}). Three properties of the benchmark shape these numbers. First, whether a
person's mailbox was collected orders ${g_custodian_inter} of the pairs that cross the collection boundary, which is
statistically indistinguishable from degree's ${g_degree_inter}. Second, two executives take part in ${ls_share} of
the pairs; weighting each manager equally lowers degree from ${g_degree_all} to ${macro_degree}. Third, the corpus
stores thousands of messages twice, once from a Lotus Notes export and once from an Outlook export whose clock is 9 to
17 hours off. A labelled check found all ${sh_labelled_shifted} sampled shifted pairs to be one message stored twice,
and the corrected deduplication rule flags ${f_probable_time_shifted_copies} copies instead of ${old_flagged}. Where the
collection does not decide the answer, on pairs of employees whose mailboxes were not collected, language-based and
weighted network measures beat degree by ${nc_gap_min} to ${nc_gap_max} points. Rebuilding every measure from random
subsets of the mailboxes shows how much the collection decides: with 25 mailboxes, degree's accuracy ranges over
${col_deg25_width} points, ${col_ratio} times the largest gap between leading measures, and collected employees rise in
the rankings. A count of the people who talk about someone in messages that person is not on is far more stable (a
${cor_talk25_width}-point range) at a cost of ${cor_talk_cost} points on the full corpus. The pipeline, tests and results
are public.</p>
</div>

<h2>1&ensp;Introduction</h2>

<p>Email records who works with whom, and researchers have long asked whether it also records who is in charge. The
Enron corpus, released during the federal investigation of the company and distributed by Carnegie Mellon University
(Cohen 2015; Klimt and Yang 2004), is the largest public collection of real workplace email, and it has served as the
test bed for inferring hierarchy from communication networks (Diesner and Carley 2005; Diesner, Frantz and Carley
2005; Creamer 2022) and from language (Bramsen et al. 2011; Gilbert 2012; Prabhakaran and Rambow 2014).</p>

<p>Agarwal, Omuya, Harnly and Rambow (2012) turned this line of work into a benchmark. They transcribed Enron's
organizational charts into a gold-standard hierarchy of dominance pairs, and showed that a person's degree in the
email network (the number of distinct people they exchanged email with) orders ${published_degree} of pairs correctly.
Agarwal, Omuya, Zhang and Rambow (2014) then showed that counting the people who are <em>mentioned</em> to someone
predicts rank better than the email network does. These numbers are the reference points for the task.</p>

<p>An accuracy figure on a benchmark mixes three things: the signal being tested, the population of pairs it is
tested on, and the data the signal is computed from. This paper rebuilds the Enron hierarchy benchmark from the raw
corpus and examines each. Our contributions are:</p>
<ol>
<li><b>A reproducible rebuild.</b> A pipeline from the raw CMU archive to every table and figure, with checksums,
unit tests and a run manifest. It reproduces the published degree baseline to within a point
(${raw_degree} against ${published_degree}).</li>
<li><b>A collection effect, measured two ways.</b> The corpus holds the mailboxes of about 150 employees (Cohen
2015). Knowing only whose mailbox was collected orders ${g_custodian_inter} of the pairs between a collected and an
uncollected employee, as well as degree does; these pairs are ${inter_share} of the main population. Rebuilding every
measure from random subsets of the mailboxes moves degree's accuracy over a ${col_deg25_width}-point range at 25
mailboxes and lifts the collected employees in the rankings.</li>
<li><b>A measure that resists the collection.</b> Counting the people who talk about someone in messages that person
is not on varies far less with the collection (a ${cor_talk25_width}-point range at 25 mailboxes) and is nearly as
accurate on the full corpus (${cor_full_talked} against ${cor_full_degree}). Two network-based corrections we tested
did not hold up.</li>
<li><b>Concentration.</b> Kenneth Lay and Jeffrey Skilling take part in ${ls_pairs} of ${main_pairs} pairs. Scoring
each manager equally, or dropping these two, changes degree's accuracy by more than the differences among the
leading measures (at most ${lead_gap} points).</li>
<li><b>Language and network compared.</b> The mention network's advantage over degree depends on the label set and
the pairs: it is clear on a title-based proxy and on uncollected employees, and not distinguishable from zero on the
main gold population, where PageRank, a purely network measure, does as well.</li>
<li><b>A data artifact.</b> Thousands of messages appear twice with timestamps 9 to 17 whole hours apart, one copy
from each of two export formats. We confirm this with a labelled sample and corpus-wide checks, and correct the
deduplication rule.</li>
</ol>

<h2>2&ensp;Related work</h2>

<p><b>Hierarchy from email networks.</b> Diesner and Carley (2005) and Diesner, Frantz and Carley (2005) described the
structure of the Enron communication network and how it changed during the company's collapse. Creamer (2022) ranks
Enron employees with a network-based corporate ranking algorithm. Agarwal et al. (2012) supplied the gold standard used
here and the degree baseline; their dominance pairs come from org charts rather than from job titles alone.</p>

<p><b>Hierarchy and power from language.</b> Bramsen et al. (2011) classified the direction of power in messages from
their language; Gilbert (2012) identified phrases that signal hierarchy in Enron email; Danescu-Niculescu-Mizil et al.
(2012) showed that people coordinate their language toward those with more power; and Prabhakaran and Rambow (2014)
predicted power relations from a single email thread. Agarwal et al. (2014) built a network from the people mentioned
in email and found that the number of different people mentioned to a person predicts their rank. Cohen, Carvalho and
Mitchell (2004) classified email into speech acts such as requests and commitments, a signal this paper does not
use but that later work on the same pipeline will.</p>

<p><b>Title lists.</b> Shetty and Adibi (2004) published a list of Enron employees with job titles, which many
studies use as a seniority label. It is undated, gives rank level rather than reporting lines, and contains duplicate
rows with conflicting titles (Diesner and Carley 2005). We use it as a secondary, noisy proxy.</p>

<h2>3&ensp;Data and pipeline</h2>

<figure>
<img src="figures/funnel.svg" alt="Data funnel">
<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> From the raw corpus to the analysis set. Percentages are of the ${f_parsed_files} files in
the CMU release. The release stores a separate file for each folder and mailbox that held a message, so removing
duplicate copies roughly halves the corpus.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>3.1&ensp;Corpus and deduplication</h3>
<p>We use the CMU release of May 7, 2015, checked against a pinned SHA-256 on every run. All ${f_parsed_files} files
parse; ${f_in_window} are dated 1998 to 2002 (Figure 1). The same message is stored many times, once in each folder
and mailbox that held it, usually under different Message-IDs. Removing copies with the same sender, timestamp,
normalized subject and normalized body, or the same Message-ID, leaves ${f_unique_messages} messages; ${f_candidate_separate_sends} messages with the same text sent to disjoint recipient
lists are kept as candidate separate sends. Recipients that appear in only some copies of a message are merged into
it. A second rule flags <em>time-shifted copies</em>, the same message stored with a timestamp shifted by a whole
number of hours. Section 7 shows that this artifact is larger than the original rule assumed, and the corrected rule
flags ${f_probable_time_shifted_copies} messages, which are kept in the data but excluded from every analysis.</p>

<h3>3.2&ensp;Authored text and exclusions</h3>
<p>Measures computed from text should read what the sender wrote, not quoted earlier messages. The pipeline estimates
each message's authored text by removing quoted replies, forwarded messages, Lotus Notes and Outlook quote headers and
legal disclaimers; ${f_with_authored_text} messages keep some authored text. We compared the cleaner with
<code>email_reply_parser</code> (Zapier's Python port of GitHub's reply parser) on ${clean_n} messages sampled with a
seed not used while writing the rules. The two agree on ${clean_agree} of messages after whitespace normalization.
Neither is ground truth, but residue counts are informative: a quoted header line is left in ${clean_hdr_ours} of our
outputs and ${clean_hdr_erp} of the reference parser's, and a &ldquo;Forwarded by&rdquo; banner in ${clean_fwd_ours} against
${clean_fwd_erp}. Our cleaner returns empty text more often (${clean_empty_ours} against ${clean_empty_erp}), mostly
for messages that only forward another.</p>
<p>The analysis set keeps messages from internal senders and excludes ${f_automated_messages} automated messages,
${f_structured_records} structured records and copied newsletters, ${f_signature_only_messages} signature-only
messages, ${f_routine_messages_excluded_from_text} long messages that their sender repeats word for word, and the
time-shifted copies. It holds ${f_analysis_messages} messages from ${f_analysis_senders} sender addresses. All flags
are rules, not a trained classifier.</p>

<h3>3.3&ensp;Senders and the communication graph</h3>
<p>Each message's sender is attributed from its own display name and address. Aliases merge on shared directory
identifiers or a go-by middle name; shared, numbered and departmental mailboxes become role keys rather than people.
${f_person_text} analysis messages (from ${f_people} people) have a sender attributed to a person. The communication
graph has a directed edge from a sender to each internal To and Cc recipient. A message to <i>n</i> internal
recipients adds 1/<i>n</i> to each of its edges, so broadcasts count less in the weighted measures; degree counts
distinct contacts and still credits broadcasts in full.</p>

<h3>3.4&ensp;Reproducibility</h3>
<p>One command (<code>make all</code>) runs every stage from download to figures. Every parameter is in one
configuration file, every random draw is seeded, and every stage records the checksums of its inputs, outputs, code
and configuration in a manifest; corpus-level checks pass only for one complete run built by the current code. The
pipeline has ${unit_tests} unit tests and ${corpus_checks} checks against the real corpus. The data layer and both evaluations went through six
independent audits by an AI code reviewer (Codex, OpenAI) before the analysis was frozen; the audit reports and the
responses to each finding are in the repository.</p>

<h2>4&ensp;Labels</h2>

<h3>4.1&ensp;Title proxy</h3>
<p>Seniority levels from the Shetty and Adibi (2004) list (CEO 6 down to trader or employee 0) are matched to
${tpeople} people in the corpus, giving ${tpairs} pairs at different levels. Two duplicate rows with conflicting titles
are dropped, following Diesner and Carley (2005), and four labels contradicted by the list's own notes or the person's
signature are flagged for a sensitivity run.</p>

<h3>4.2&ensp;Gold standard</h3>
<p>The gold standard comes from a release that the authors of Agarwal et al. (2012) share on request: org-chart
positions joined through organizational units. An employee dominates another when a chain of units leads from one of
their positions to one of the other's; the dominance pairs are the transitive closure of these relations, restricted
to the ${gold_with_email} of ${gold_employees} employee records that have email addresses. Our reconstruction yields
${gold_pairs} pairs; the paper reports ${paper_pairs} from ${paper_relations} immediate relations. The release does
not say which construction the paper used, so two alternatives are kept as sensitivity runs (positions closed before
mapping to employees, ${positions_first_pairs} pairs; arcs of the one contradictory cycle removed first,
${cycle_pairs} pairs). Our results are therefore related to the paper's, not a replication of them.</p>

<p>Employees are matched to graph nodes by their principal name rather than their listed addresses, because the
release often lists an executive's assistant's address under the executive. Of the ${gold_with_email} employees,
${match_name_address} match by name with a confirming address, ${match_name} by name only and ${match_address_node} to
an address spelled from their name; ${match_ambiguous} are ambiguous and ${match_absent} absent. ${mixed_records}
records merge an assistant's position with another position, and ${multi_records} hold addresses that resolve to
several people. The <b>main population</b> is the ${main_pairs} pairs whose two employees are matched and whose
dominance does not start, end or run through one of the ${mixed_records} merged records. A stricter population that
also excludes the multi-person records has ${strict_pairs} pairs.</p>

<h3>4.3&ensp;Pair types</h3>
<p>Following Agarwal et al. (2012), pairs are split by whether the two employees are <i>custodians</i>, people whose
mailboxes were collected: <i>core</i> pairs join two custodians, <i>inter</i> pairs a custodian and a non-custodian,
and <i>non-core</i> pairs two non-custodians. Over all ${gold_pairs} reconstructed pairs the split is ${full_core} /
${full_inter} / ${full_noncore} (the paper reports ${paper_core} / ${paper_inter} / ${paper_noncore}); in the main
population it is ${gpairs_core} / ${gpairs_inter} / ${gpairs_noncore}.</p>

<h2>5&ensp;Measures and evaluation</h2>

<p><b>Network measures.</b> Degree (distinct contacts, undirected), weighted email received (in-strength), messages
sent (out-strength), PageRank on the weighted directed graph (Brin and Page 1998; damping 0.85) and exact betweenness
(Freeman 1977; Brandes 2001) with edge length 1/weight.</p>

<p><b>Mention network.</b> Following Agarwal et al. (2014), person names are tagged in the first 5,000 characters of
each message's authored text with spaCy's <code>en_core_web_sm</code> model (Honnibal et al. 2020), in place of the
AceJet tagger the paper used. For each recipient of a message, a name resolves to the compatible person (by full name,
first name or nickname, last name or initials) closest to both sender and recipient in the email network; ties and
unreachable names are left unresolved. This yields ${m_links} resolved (message, recipient, name) links. The main
measures count only messages from a person and leave out names that resolve to the sender (${m_self}, mostly signatures
and contact lines), names after an office title on the same line (&ldquo;Governor Davis&rdquo;, ${m_office}) and before a company
word (&ldquo;Williams pipeline&rdquo;, ${m_company}), keeping ${m_main}. We report three measures: <i>mention-network degree</i>
(the paper's best system), <i>people mentioned to them</i> (the number of different people mentioned to a person,
declared our primary mention measure) and <i>others mentioned to them</i> (the same, without the sender and the
message's recipients). The paper's consistency check (when exactly one compatible person is Cc'd, take them as the
answer) agrees with our resolver on ${cc_acc} of ${cc_checks} cases (the paper reports ${published_cc_check}).</p>

<p><b>Custodian baseline.</b> A score of 1 for employees whose mailbox is in the corpus and 0 otherwise. It uses no
communication at all, so it measures how much of the task is about which mailboxes were collected.</p>

<p><b>Scoring.</b> Accuracy is the share of dominance pairs in which the measure ranks the dominant employee higher;
ties count as half, so chance is 50%. Intervals are 95% percentile intervals from 1,000 bootstrap resamples of people,
not pairs, since pairs that share a person are not independent; measures are compared by paired differences on the
same resamples. These intervals cover sampling of people only, not matching, label or graph-construction choices,
which the sensitivity runs address.</p>

<h2>6&ensp;Results</h2>

<h3>6.1&ensp;Reproducing the baseline</h3>
<p>Agarwal et al. (2012) computed degree over a graph in which aliases were merged into people. Computing degree over
raw addresses in our windowed, deduplicated mail, taking each employee's highest-degree release address, over all
${gold_pairs} pairs gives ${raw_degree}, against the published
${published_degree}. Given the differences in pair construction, we take this as a successful reproduction of the
baseline's level.</p>

