<p>Table 1 and Figure 2 give the main results. On the gold standard, PageRank (${g_pagerank_all}) and the three mention
measures (${g_mention_degree_all} to ${g_third_party_mentioned_to_all}) lead, degree follows at ${g_degree_all},
and betweenness and messages sent trail. The title proxy orders the measures differently: mention-network degree leads
at ${t_mention_degree} (paired difference with degree ${td_mention_degree_degree} points,
${tdci_mention_degree_degree}), while PageRank (${t_pagerank}) falls below degree. Which measure is &ldquo;best&rdquo; therefore depends on the label set.</p>

<h3>6.3&ensp;Much of the task is which mailboxes were collected</h3>

<figure>
<img src="figures/pair_types.svg" alt="Accuracy by pair type">
<figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Accuracy by pair type in the main gold population. When one employee's mailbox was
collected and the other's was not (middle), knowing whose mailbox was collected (grey) orders pairs about as well as
degree. When neither or both were collected, the custodian baseline cannot separate the pair and scores 50%.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The collected mailboxes belong mostly to Enron's senior managers (Cohen 2015), and a custodian's whole mailbox
adds their mail to the graph, so custodians have high degree almost by construction. On the ${gpairs_inter} inter pairs (${inter_share} of the main population) the custodian baseline orders
${g_custodian_inter} correctly; degree orders ${g_degree_inter}, a paired difference of ${d_custodian_inter} points
(${dci_custodian_inter}). On these pairs the benchmark largely measures the collection: every measure except messages
sent scores between ${inter_lo} and ${inter_hi} (Figure 3, Table 1). The informative pairs are the ${gpairs_noncore} non-core pairs (${noncore_share} of the
population), where neither mailbox was collected and each person appears only through others' mail. There degree falls
to ${g_degree_noncore}, and the measures separate. Section 7 varies the collection itself.</p>

