<h3>6.4&ensp;Two executives carry a quarter of the pairs</h3>
<p>Dominance pairs are the transitive closure of an org chart, so a person near the top dominates everyone below
them. Kenneth Lay and Jeffrey Skilling take part in ${ls_pairs} of the ${main_pairs} main pairs (${ls_share}). Every
measure ranks them near the top, so they inflate accuracy. Dropping the pairs that involve them lowers degree from
${g_degree_all} to ${no_ls_degree}. Averaging accuracy over dominant employees, so that each manager counts once,
lowers it to ${macro_degree} (PageRank ${macro_pagerank}, people mentioned to them ${macro_mentioned_to}). These shifts
are larger than any difference between the leading measures.</p>

<h3>6.5&ensp;Language against network</h3>
<p>On the main population, the primary mention measure's difference from degree is ${d_mentioned_to_all} points
(${dci_mentioned_to_all}) and mention-network degree's is ${d_mention_degree_all} (${dci_mention_degree_all}); neither
is distinguishable from zero. For others mentioned to them, which removes the sender and recipients, it is
${d_third_party_mentioned_to_all} (${dci_third_party_mentioned_to_all}), and for PageRank ${d_pagerank_all}
(${dci_pagerank_all}). The picture changes on non-core pairs, where the collection cannot decide the answer: the
differences from degree are ${d_mentioned_to_noncore} for people mentioned to them (${dci_mentioned_to_noncore}),
${d_third_party_mentioned_to_noncore} for others mentioned to them (${dci_third_party_mentioned_to_noncore}) and
${d_pagerank_noncore} for PageRank (${dci_pagerank_noncore}) (Table 2). Language helps most where communication volume is observed
only indirectly, but on this benchmark it does about as well as a weighted network measure, not clearly better.</p>
<p>One caution applies. The primary mention measure was declared after unfiltered mention results were known but
before the filters for signatures, office titles and company names were run; the filtered comparison is therefore
exploratory. Without the filters, people mentioned to them scores ${g_mentioned_to_unfiltered_all}, a difference from
degree of ${d_mentioned_to_unfiltered_all} (${dci_mentioned_to_unfiltered_all}).</p>

<h3>6.6&ensp;Sensitivity</h3>

<figure>
<img src="figures/sensitivity.svg" alt="Sensitivity to population and scoring">
<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Accuracy of degree, PageRank, people mentioned to them and the custodian baseline under
each gold-standard population and scoring (pairs in parentheses). The shaded row is the main population. The same
measure moves across a range far wider than the gaps between degree, PageRank and people mentioned to them within any
