</figure>

<p>Figure 4 repeats the evaluation under nine alternative populations and scorings: stricter and looser handling of
uncertain records, matching only by name and address, the two alternative pair constructions, dropping Lay and
Skilling, averaging per manager, and the convention of scoring unmatched employees as zero over all pairs. Degree
ranges from ${degree_min} to ${degree_max}. The primary mention measure is ahead of degree in all ${sens_variants}
runs, by ${sens_gap_min} to ${sens_gap_max} points. On the title proxy, degree moves between ${t_degree_no_ceo} (CEOs
and presidents removed) and ${t_degree_people_only} (people-only graph); building the graph only from messages with at
most 10 recipients gives ${t_degree_max10}, so broadcasts account for part of degree's signal.</p>

<h2>7&ensp;How much does the collection decide?</h2>

<p>Section 6.3 showed that knowing whose mailbox was collected orders most of the pairs that cross the collection
boundary. The corpus itself is one collection: the ${col_mailboxes} or so mailboxes that investigators gathered. To ask how much a
different collection would change the results, we rebuilt every measure from chosen subsets of the mailboxes. The
design, outcomes and expectations below were written down and committed to the project's repository before any of
them was computed (<code>collection/PLAN.md</code> and <code>collection/PLAN_corrections.md</code>).</p>

<h3>7.1&ensp;Design</h3>
<p>A collection keeps every message that has at least one stored copy in one of its mailboxes, counting exact
duplicates and time-shifted copies, and nothing else. Degree, PageRank and the mention measures are rebuilt from those
messages with the rules of Sections 3 and 5 (betweenness is left out for time) and scored on the fixed main population
of ${main_pairs} pairs; an employee absent from a collection's graph scores zero, as an analyst holding only that
collection would see them. On all ${col_mailboxes} mailboxes the rebuild reproduces the accuracies of Section 6 exactly. We
drew ${col_draws} random collections at each of ${col_sizes} mailboxes, and three ordered ones at each size: the busiest
mailboxes first, the most senior custodians first (those who dominate the most gold employees, as an investigator
working from the org chart might choose) and the most junior first, ${col_collections} collections in all. Besides
accuracy, we measured collection bias, the change in collected employees' percentile rank compared with the full
corpus, and how many of the full corpus's 20 highest-ranked employees stay in a collection's top 20.</p>

<h3>7.2&ensp;Which mailboxes are collected matters more than which measure is used</h3>
<p>With 25 random mailboxes, degree orders between ${col_deg25_lo} and ${col_deg25_hi} of pairs depending on which 25
were drawn (95% range; median ${col_deg25_median}), a spread of ${col_deg25_width} points, ${col_ratio} times the largest
difference between leading measures on the full corpus (${lead_gap} points). With 10 mailboxes it ranges from
${col_deg10_lo} to ${col_deg10_hi} (Figure 5a). The collection also changes who looks important. Collected employees
rank higher by PageRank in their collection than in the full corpus in ${col_bias_share10} of 10-mailbox draws and
${col_bias_share25} of 25-mailbox draws, by a median ${col_pr_bias10} percentile points at 10 mailboxes (Figure 5b). A
typical 25-mailbox collection keeps ${col_top20_25} of the full corpus's 20 highest-ranked employees in its own top 20
(${col_top20_50} at 50 mailboxes, ${col_top20_100} at 100), and the rank correlation with the full corpus rises from
${col_spear10} at 10 mailboxes to ${col_spear125} at 125. Choosing senior custodians first beats the median random
collection at ${col_senior_sizes} of 6 sizes (${col_senior10} against ${col_random10} at 10 mailboxes) but not at 100
(${col_senior100} against ${col_random100}), so that expectation is not met (Table 3).</p>

<figure>
<img src="figures/collection.svg" alt="Accuracy and rank shift by number of mailboxes collected">
<figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Measures rebuilt from random collections of the 150 mailboxes (${col_draws} draws per
size; lines are medians, bands 95% ranges; the point at 150 is the full corpus). (a) Accuracy on the ${main_pairs}
main pairs. (b) Collection bias: the median change in collected employees' percentile rank compared with the same
measure on the full corpus. Degree and PageRank lift the collected; anchored degree and talked about do not, and talked
about's accuracy varies least.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>7.3&ensp;Measures that resist the collection</h3>
<p>The cause is visibility: a collected employee's mail is seen in full, an uncollected employee's only through the
collected. We tested three measures built so that being collected adds nothing. <b>Anchored degree</b> counts the
distinct collection owners a person exchanged mail with; every message with an owner on it is in the collection, so
these ties are visible for everyone. <b>Anchored strength</b> weights the same ties by message volume. <b>Talked
about</b> counts the distinct people who mention a person in messages that person is not on; whether such a message is
collected depends on its sender and recipients, not on the person mentioned. A mailbox's owner is the person on the
most of its messages, which assigns Skilling's mailbox to Skilling rather than to the assistant who sent from it.</p>
<p>Anchored degree removes most of the bias from 25 mailboxes up but overcorrects at 10 (a median shift of
${cor_bias10_anchored} percentile points, against ${cor_bias10_degree} for degree), and its accuracy varies more than
degree's (${cor_anch25_width} against ${col_deg25_width} points at 25 mailboxes), so two of its expectations fail. Talked
about is closer to neutral than degree at every size (${cor_bias10_talked} at 10 mailboxes, ${cor_bias25_talked} at 25,
against ${cor_bias10_degree} and ${cor_bias25_degree}). Beyond the stated expectations, and therefore descriptively, it
is also the most stable measure: at 25 mailboxes its accuracy ranges from ${cor_talk25_lo} to ${cor_talk25_hi}
(${cor_talk25_width} points, against ${col_deg25_width} for degree), at 10 from ${cor_talk10_lo} to ${cor_talk10_hi},
and it keeps ${cor_top20_25_talked} of the full corpus's top 20 at 25 mailboxes, against ${cor_top20_25_degree} for
degree and ${cor_top20_25_pagerank} for PageRank. Its cost on the full corpus is ${cor_talk_cost} points
(${cor_full_talked} against ${cor_full_degree}). Anchored strength is less accurate throughout (${cor_full_strength}
on the full corpus).</p>

