<h3>7.4&ensp;Implications for collected corpora</h3>
<p>Every corpus assembled for an investigation or a lawsuit is a collection. In electronic discovery the parties
choose custodians, and any analysis of the produced mail inherits that choice. On this benchmark, a different choice of
25 custodians moves a standard measure of rank over a ${col_deg25_width}-point range and replaces half or more of the 20
people it ranks highest, while a count of who is talked about, rather than who emails whom, moves over ${cor_talk25_width} points.
Results computed on a collected corpus should report how they change when custodians are resampled, and measures that
do not depend on a person's own mailbox are the safer basis for conclusions about who mattered.</p>

<h2>8&ensp;Time-shifted copies: one message, two clocks</h2>

<p>While building a semantic search over the corpus (sentence embeddings from <code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code>; Reimers
and Gurevych 2019; stored in PostgreSQL with pgvector), we ran an exact similarity join over pairs of messages from the
same person sent within 48 hours. It surfaced ${sh_long_pairs} pairs whose authored text, subject and recipients are
identical and whose timestamps differ by a whole 9 to 17 hours, beyond the 8-hour maximum of the original
time-shifted-copy rule, and ${sh_near_pairs} pairs whose gap misses a whole number of hours by less than a minute
(${sh_near_5} of them about 5 hours). Labellers in an earlier pilot had also flagged a few such pairs. Were these two
messages, or one message stored twice?</p>

<div class="tbl">
<div class="tcap"><b>Table 4.</b> Labelled check of candidate pairs. Pairs were drawn with a fixed seed (long-shift
pairs stratified by gap), and each was judged from its raw headers, the folder and export file of every copy, and
quote headers in other messages that cite it. Ten controls, same-sender pairs whose gap is not near a whole number of
hours, were mixed in. Intervals are Wilson 95% intervals (Wilson 1927).</div>
<table>
<tr><th>Frame</th><th>Pairs</th><th>Labelled</th><th>One message stored twice</th><th>One export folder holds both</th></tr>
<tr><td>A whole 9 to 17 hours apart</td><td>${sh_long_pairs}</td><td>${sh_long_n}</td><td>${sh_long_k} <span class=ci>(${sh_long_ci})</span></td><td>${sh_long_both}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Within a minute of whole hours</td><td>${sh_near_pairs}</td><td>${sh_near_n}</td><td>${sh_near_k} <span class=ci>(${sh_near_ci})</span></td><td>${sh_near_both}</td></tr>
<tr><td>Controls</td><td>${sh_control_pairs}</td><td>${sh_control_n}</td><td>${sh_control_k} <span class=ci>(${sh_control_ci})</span></td><td>${sh_control_both}</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<p>All ${sh_labelled_shifted} sampled shifted pairs were judged to be one message stored twice, and all ten controls two
separate sends (Table 4). Checks over every candidate pair, not only the sample, point the same way.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Two exports.</b> The CMU release stores each mailbox's mail from one or more export files: Lotus Notes
(<code>.nsf</code>) or Outlook (<code>.pst</code>). In none of the ${sh_long_pairs} long-shift pairs does one folder of
one export hold both messages, whereas ${sh_control_both} of the ${sh_control_pairs} controls do. In ${sh_long_notes}
of the ${sh_long_pairs}, the earlier message's copies all come from a Notes export and the later message's from an
Outlook export.</li>
<li><b>Daylight saving time.</b> The gap moves by an hour with US daylight saving time: all ${sh_10_total} pairs exactly
10 hours apart were sent in daylight time, and ${sh_11_standard} of the ${sh_11_total} pairs 11 hours apart in
standard time (Figure 6). This is consistent with one copy's clock ignoring daylight saving time.</li>
<li><b>Not chance.</b> From the controls' gaps, two separate sends would land this close to a whole number of hours
about ${sh_chance_whole} times (on whole hours) or ${sh_chance_near} times (within a minute) by chance, against
${sh_observed} pairs observed.</li>
</ul>

<figure>
<img src="figures/shift.svg" alt="Time-shifted pairs by gap and clock">
<figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Candidate time-shifted pairs by the gap between their two timestamps, split by whether the
message was sent during US daylight time (orange) or standard time (blue). Exact 10-hour gaps occur only in daylight
