</figure>

<p><b>Correction.</b> The deduplication rule now flags a later message with the same sender, normalized subject,
normalized body (over 100 characters) and recipients as another message, sent 1 to 17 whole hours after it, give or
take under 60 seconds. It flags ${f_probable_time_shifted_copies} messages instead of ${old_flagged}, removing
${analysis_removed} more from the analysis set; raising the maximum to 48 hours would add ${max48_extra}. It catches
${caught_long} of the ${sh_long_pairs} long-shift pairs, ${caught_near} of the ${sh_near_pairs} near-whole-hour pairs, and none of the controls; the pairs it misses differ in quoted text below the authored text or
in how the sender's address is written, are too short, or (4 long-shift pairs) are a note sent twice minutes apart with
each send stored twice, since the rule compares each copy only with the one before it. The correction changes every benchmark accuracy on the main
populations by at most ${delta_main} points, and by at most ${delta_any} points within any pair type, so the results
above do not depend on it.</p>

<p><b>Which clock is right?</b> Quote headers in other messages date ${q_dated} of the labelled pairs. In the ${q_long}
long-shift pairs among them, the quoted send time falls between the two copies, in ${q_halfway} of them within an hour of
halfway: the Notes copy is early and the Outlook copy late. In the ${q_near} near-whole-hour pairs, the quoted time matches the copy whose
timestamp includes seconds. Timestamps in some exports are therefore hours off. This does not affect the
counts-based measures here, but it matters for any measure of reply timing, and it is not yet corrected.</p>

<h2>9&ensp;Limitations</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Labels from AI labellers.</b> The time-shifted copy check was labelled by one AI labeller (Claude, Anthropic)
following written guidelines, and a pilot check of other pipeline components (below) by two AI agents (Codex, OpenAI)
that settled their disagreements from the evidence. Labellers built on the same model can share blind spots; these
are estimates until a human-labelled subset confirms them.</li>
<li><b>Pipeline accuracy.</b> In that pilot, on the run before the copy correction, the parent of a reply link was
correct for ${p_high} of high-confidence links (n=${p_high_n}), ${p_medium} of medium (n=${p_medium_n}) and ${p_low} of
low (n=${p_low_n}), about ${p_precision} weighted by link counts. Names in the mention measures resolved to the right
person ${p_mention} of the time (n=${p_mention_n}; ${p_mention_ci}); ${p_mention_outside} of the ${p_mention_wrong}
errors were people outside Enron matched to an employee with the same name. Signatures, rosters and forwarded text
inside messages still contribute names.</li>
<li><b>The gold standard.</b> Our reconstruction does not reproduce the paper's pair counts, matching employees to the
graph relies on rules, and the release cannot be redistributed, so others need to request it from its authors.</li>
<li><b>The title proxy</b> is undated and gives rank level only.</li>
<li><b>The collection experiment</b> resamples the investigators' mailboxes, not the company's full mail. Name
resolution for the mention measures was done on the full corpus, so a real smaller collection would resolve fewer
names; collection owners are inferred from the mail (the weakest inference covers ${owner_weakest} of its mailbox's
messages); betweenness was left out; and the senior-first collections are chosen with the gold labels, so their
accuracy is not independent of them. Talked about's stability was observed, not predicted in advance.</li>
<li><b>Scope.</b> One company, 1998 to 2002, a small spaCy model in place of the original tagger, and runs reproduced
on macOS only.</li>
</ul>

<h2>10&ensp;Conclusion</h2>
<p>Email does reveal who outranks whom at Enron: every reasonable measure orders most dominance pairs correctly. But
the benchmark's headline accuracies say less about communication than they appear to. In the main population,
${inter_share} of pairs cross the collection boundary and can be ordered by knowing whose mailbox was collected, ${ls_share} involve two
executives, and the same measure moves by ${degree_span} points across defensible choices of population and scoring. The
collection matters most: a different choice of 25 mailboxes moves degree's accuracy over a ${col_deg25_width}-point range,
more than six times the gaps between leading measures, and lifts whoever was collected. Comparisons between measures
are most informative on pairs of uncollected employees, where language-based and weighted network measures both beat
degree, and a count of who is talked about holds up across collections far better than counts of who emails whom.</p>
<p>We recommend that future work on this benchmark, and analyses of any collected corpus, report results by pair type,
report a per-manager average alongside the pooled accuracy, include the custodian baseline, show how results change
when custodians are resampled, and deduplicate time-shifted export copies before measuring anything from timestamps.
This work is part of a larger project that treats formal rank and functional importance, how much others depend on a
person, as separate quantities; a companion study of employees' absences is in preparation.</p>

